(5609144, 811750, 100335, '<td valign="top">Sweet!<br/> <br/>Edit: Pun was NOT intended!<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2009-08-12 13:12:27.</i></td>', 'Sweet! Edit: Pun was NOT intended!', '2009-08-12 14:11:59.000000', '2009-08-12 14:12:27.000000', 0),
(5609159, 811959, 68956, '<td valign="top">Hi Doug from the <b>Critique Club</b>!<br/> <br/><b>First impressions</b> <br/>Oooh!<br/> <br/><b>After a bit of consideration</b> <br/>Your image is colourful and well composed, each area acts as an image in it''s own right giving your eyes a real feast! There is the problem of entering rectangles in a square challenge and I suspect that might have lost you a bit on your score.<br/> <br/><b>Overall</b> <br/>You''ve seen something that''s interesting to look at, framed it in your camera and shared it with the world, and that to me is great photography. Well done on your first challenge entry and I hope there will be many more :)<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2009-08-12 13:25:44.</i></td>', 'Hi Doug from the Critique Club! First impressions Oooh! After a bit of consideration Your image is colourful and well composed, each area acts as an image in it''s own right giving your eyes a real feast! There is the problem of entering rectangles in a square challenge and I suspect that might have lost you a bit on your score. Overall You''ve seen something that''s interesting to look at, framed it in your camera and shared it with the world, and that to me is great photography. Well done on your first challenge entry and I hope there will be many more :)', '2009-08-12 14:23:26.000000', '2009-08-12 14:25:44.000000', 0),
(5609173, 810740, 811, '<td valign="top">I get the concept -- unique and a good take on it.  Could have turned the dice to both showing "4" to make it even more "squarish."<br/> <br/>I''d say your score was due more to the lighting and focus issues of the shot, more than the concept.<br/> <br/>Good idea, though.</td>', 'I get the concept -- unique and a good take on it.  Could have turned the dice to both showing "4" to make it even more "squarish." I''d say your score was due more to the lighting and focus issues of the shot, more than the concept. Good idea, though.', '2009-08-12 14:31:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(5609339, 811716, 89763, '<td valign="top">I like it off-centered as it is. :o)</td>', 'I like it off-centered as it is. :o)', '2009-08-12 17:47:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(5609349, 811750, 16912, '<td valign="top">Greetings from Andi via the Critique Club<br/> <br/><b>First Impression:</b> My first thought was that this was a map of Milton Keynes! Seriously this was one of my highest scoring images and the red cube (not square) bumped it to an 8 from my speed vote of 6.<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> Without my set square this is about as perfect as you can get and I''m pretty sure if I''d have thought of this myself the red cube would have been centered and so ruined the overall effect of the shot<br/> <br/><b>Subject:</b> ok, I guess a cube is made up of 6 squares so it meets the challenge many (many) times. For a while I thought you had the cubes sitting in liquid and it was being sucked up but that would be almost impossible, it was then I realised it was a reflection. Simple and beautiful yet complex at the same time.<br/> <br/><b>Technical:</b> Can''t fault the technicals here as they are pretty much perfect. there is a lot of blue in the image and whilst I like it I wonder how a selective desaturation would have worked? B&amp;W &amp; Red?<br/> <br/><b>Final thoughts:</b> A great score for a great image with a good place to boot! Time well spent on the shopping and setting up Chris!</td>', 'Greetings from Andi via the Critique Club First Impression: My first thought was that this was a map of Milton Keynes! Seriously this was one of my highest scoring images and the red cube (not square) bumped it to an 8 from my speed vote of 6. Composition: Without my set square this is about as perfect as you can get and I''m pretty sure if I''d have thought of this myself the red cube would have been centered and so ruined the overall effect of the shot Subject: ok, I guess a cube is made up of 6 squares so it meets the challenge many (many) times. For a while I thought you had the cubes sitting in liquid and it was being sucked up but that would be almost impossible, it was then I realised it was a reflection. Simple and beautiful yet complex at the same time. Technical: Can''t fault the technicals here as they are pretty much perfect. there is a lot of blue in the image and whilst I like it I wonder how a selective desaturation would have worked? B&W & Red? Final thoughts: A great score for a great image with a good place to boot! Time well spent on the shopping and setting up Chris!', '2009-08-12 17:56:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(5609806, 811876, 23098, '<td valign="top">Congrats Andrew, cool shot!</td>', 'Congrats Andrew, cool shot!', '2009-08-13 00:14:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(5609826, 811826, 50695, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Konador:</b></div><hr/><i> I wouldn''t say the main subject was square-shaped here, sorry. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Just wondering how much you learned about photography from that comment...</td>', 'Originally posted by Konador: I wouldn''t say the main subject was square-shaped here, sorry.   Just wondering how much you learned about photography from that comment...', '2009-08-13 00:26:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(5610016, 812000, 68956, '<td valign="top"><u><b>Critique Club</b></u> <br/> <br/>Hi Robin :)<br/> <br/><b>First impressions</b> <br/>A dynamic, colourful image.<br/> <br/><b>After some time with the image</b> <br/>You''ve created an image full of magic that fits in well with the theme, the set up and composition are good. Whilst the lights do add a great deal to the image I can see that all those circles within the confines of a square challenge could have lost you a few points. You mention focus, specifically the cube, it''s difficult to tell but with the image at this size the focus seems fine and any drop off in sharpness is probably due to the small depth of field that you would have got with an aperture of f1.8. <br/>A commenter mentioned the blurry hand and that is probably more of an issue, motion blur would have been fine but this looks like movement blur of a hungry husband expected to keep still for 1/50sec ;)<br/> <br/><b>Overall</b> <br/>A creative image that has worked well, you were constrained by time and the prospect of basic editing, so you can only improve on this already good image.<br/>:)</td>', 'Critique Club  Hi Robin :) First impressions A dynamic, colourful image. After some time with the image You''ve created an image full of magic that fits in well with the theme, the set up and composition are good. Whilst the lights do add a great deal to the image I can see that all those circles within the confines of a square challenge could have lost you a few points. You mention focus, specifically the cube, it''s difficult to tell but with the image at this size the focus seems fine and any drop off in sharpness is probably due to the small depth of field that you would have got with an aperture of f1.8. A commenter mentioned the blurry hand and that is probably more of an issue, motion blur would have been fine but this looks like movement blur of a hungry husband expected to keep still for 1/50sec ;) Overall A creative image that has worked well, you were constrained by time and the prospect of basic editing, so you can only improve on this already good image.:)', '2009-08-13 06:08:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(5610039, 811085, 79955, '<td valign="top">Kok Weng , nice shot !! lovely contrast and well captured.<br/>Don''t worry too much about the score, it just means that the subject matter did not really make a big impact (interest) to half the voters.</td>', 'Kok Weng , nice shot !! lovely contrast and well captured.Don''t worry too much about the score, it just means that the subject matter did not really make a big impact (interest) to half the voters.', '2009-08-13 07:08:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(5610043, 812032, 74588, '<td valign="top">O</td>', 'O', '2009-08-13 07:19:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(5610090, 811850, 77196, '<td valign="top">You might try a 2nd light source, on the opposite side. In this shot, your lighting is semi-strong and from the left only. It fades quickly so the back of the board is too dark (the black pieces almost fade into the background). Also, it makes strong shadows and hot spots on the various pieces. <br/>Your arrangement of the pieces is very nice, and the shot invites one in to play the game. <br/></td>', 'You might try a 2nd light source, on the opposite side. In this shot, your lighting is semi-strong and from the left only. It fades quickly so the back of the board is too dark (the black pieces almost fade into the background). Also, it makes strong shadows and hot spots on the various pieces. Your arrangement of the pieces is very nice, and the shot invites one in to play the game. ', '2009-08-13 08:32:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(5610120, 811876, 80868, '<td valign="top">I don''t know about everyone else, but on my monitor, as I gaze at the central skylight, the picture seems to be moving towards me. Freaky cool. What a great and active picture.</td>', 'I don''t know about everyone else, but on my monitor, as I gaze at the central skylight, the picture seems to be moving towards me. Freaky cool. What a great and active picture.', '2009-08-13 09:14:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(5610325, 811910, 63573, '<td valign="top">Outstanding concept. Fun too. Well done.</td>', 'Outstanding concept. Fun too. Well done.', '2009-08-13 12:37:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(5610329, 810740, 87808, '<td valign="top">HI from the CC Club, <br/> <br/>I laughed when I saw your title, fits perfectly. <br/> <br/>I like the fact that you have humour going on, and the squares and the roots, it all adds up.<br/> <br/>The first thing that I notice is that your shot is very small, remember you can use up to 640 on one side in these shots, so that they are bigger.<br/> <br/>the second thing is your lighting.  It is very harsh.  When you are lightpainting something this small you will want your seconds to be a lot smaller.  Here are a couple of 30 seconds lightpainting shots, this will give you an idea as to what you can achieve with 30 seconds.  Also, with lightpainting it depends on what size light you have going on here.  The below shots where done with a flashlight in a tube with some tissue stuffed into it to diffuse it. <br/>.<a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=648508" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/813/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_648508.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/813/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_648508.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/813/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_648508.jpg"/></a> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=629597" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/85000-89999/87808/120/629597.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/85000-89999/87808/120/629597.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/85000-89999/87808/120/629597.jpg"/></a> <br/> <br/>Next your focus is a little off.  When you are doing light painting it is usually done in manual mode which you have done. <br/> <br/>Set up your shot, in normal whatever normal mode you use. with the lights on, focus, then flick your lens from  AF to MF, go to manual mode.  Then shoot.  this will ensure crisp sharp focus.  and always remember, paint what you want the camera to see. <br/> <br/>You are also tilted, this can be seen as you have some of the wall and table meeting, straight this out.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2009-08-13 11:44:08.</i></td>', 'HI from the CC Club,  I laughed when I saw your title, fits perfectly.  I like the fact that you have humour going on, and the squares and the roots, it all adds up. The first thing that I notice is that your shot is very small, remember you can use up to 640 on one side in these shots, so that they are bigger. the second thing is your lighting.  It is very harsh.  When you are lightpainting something this small you will want your seconds to be a lot smaller.  Here are a couple of 30 seconds lightpainting shots, this will give you an idea as to what you can achieve with 30 seconds.  Also, with lightpainting it depends on what size light you have going on here.  The below shots where done with a flashlight in a tube with some tissue stuffed into it to diffuse it. .   Next your focus is a little off.  When you are doing light painting it is usually done in manual mode which you have done.  Set up your shot, in normal whatever normal mode you use. with the lights on, focus, then flick your lens from  AF to MF, go to manual mode.  Then shoot.  this will ensure crisp sharp focus.  and always remember, paint what you want the camera to see.  You are also tilted, this can be seen as you have some of the wall and table meeting, straight this out.', '2009-08-13 12:39:11.000000', '2009-08-13 12:44:08.000000', 0),
(5610494, 811826, 101668, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by posthumous:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Konador:</b></div><hr/><i> I wouldn''t say the main subject was square-shaped here, sorry. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Just wondering how much you learned about photography from that comment... </i></td></tr></table> <br/>I consider every comment carefully and then decide. This comment was much better than a previous "oof"! I could not,for the life of me, learn anything from that! Thank you for your comment. I do try to be a better photographer.</td>', 'Originally posted by posthumous: Originally posted by Konador: I wouldn''t say the main subject was square-shaped here, sorry.   Just wondering how much you learned about photography from that comment...  I consider every comment carefully and then decide. This comment was much better than a previous "oof"! I could not,for the life of me, learn anything from that! Thank you for your comment. I do try to be a better photographer.', '2009-08-13 14:56:08.000000', NULL, 0),
